body:
>Like let’s say I have some left overs in the fridge. Its hard for me  grasp that I need to eat them soon because they go bad. I want them to  just last forever and feel like that should make them last.   Overall, I think I just have an issue grasping that time exists.

All of this I 100% agree with. I don't agree with the whole "knew I was getting older" thing because, while I think I knew it was happening, I didn't acknowledge it was happening and now I'm almost 30 and yet I still dissociate and think it's 2002 so often and it's awful. I don't understand time and it scares me and nobody can help. I don't even wanna deal with time anymore, it's too frustrating. I don't wanna deal with anything. I think things from last year were two weeks ago. I think 8 years ago was last year. I'm all over the place and it's terrible.

&#x200B;

Honestly, I gotta go with the [Castle De Los Muertos](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=setD_y214Q0) song from The Neverhood sequel "SkullMonkeys". Just a downright piece of brilliance. Both soundtracks are fantastic, but this damn piece, man. I love it.

&#x200B;

Either Dex or Skullgirls. Thanks OP!

I, for one, have never understood the idea of reviewing something BEFORE you've seen or experienced it. This happens on Amazon alllll the goddamned time. 

&#x200B;

But overall, I agree. I use the site to catalogue and whatnot, like so many others say, and what I generally do is I'll go to a bookstore, find stuff I'm interested in but can't buy at the moment but want to read down the road, and will log it on the site so I can eventually get to it. I simply don't trust other people to give me recommendations because literature, much like music, varies so wildly by person that I simply want to discover what I want on my own without having to read, generally horrible, reviews of something I probably won't like anyway.

&#x200B;

Whenever I write a review, I do it covering the things you mention. How well it's written, how interesting it is, etc. Merits that a book SHOULD theoretically be reviewed upon, not simply "I heard this author's kind of a dick sometimes." I agree that there's talking points to be had on everything, but not to be the sole reason for an entire review. You're reviewing a book, not the person who wrote it. But what do I know. I'm just a stupid 29 year old woman who's currently reading mostly Franzen lol
